===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make a ruffle in; to curl or [[flute]], ''as'' an edge of fabric.
#: {{ux|en|Ruffle the end of the cuff.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[disturb]]; especially, to cause to [[flutter]].
#: {{ux|en|The wind '''ruffled''' the papers.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her sudden volley of insults '''ruffled''' his composure.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Rees|title=Finn Russell masterminds historic Scotland victory over England|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2018/feb/24/scotland-england-six-nations-match-report|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180422092213/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2018/feb/24/scotland-england-six-nations-match-report|archivedate=22 April 2018|newspaper={{w|The Guardian}}|location=London|date=24 February 2018|passage=[[w:Finn Russell|[Finn] Russell]] created his side’s three first-half tries, '''ruffling''' a defence known for its composure, and dictated the match from the off.}}
#* '''1832''', {{w|Isaac Taylor}}, ''Saturday Evening''
#*: the fantastic revelries {{...}} that so often '''ruffled''' the placid bosom of the Nile
#* '''1860''', {{w|Sir William Hamilton, 9th Baronet|Sir William Hamilton}}, ''Sir William Hamilton, 9th Baronet''
#*: These '''ruffle''' the tranquillity of the mind.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Palamon and Arcite}}
#*: She {{...}}smoothed the '''ruffled''' seas.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Guinevere}}
#*: But, ever after, the small violence done / Rankled in him and '''ruffled''' all his heart.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To grow rough, boisterous, or turbulent.
#*{{RQ:Shakespeare Lear|II|iv|passage=The night comes on, and the bleak winds / Do sorely '''ruffle'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become disordered; to play loosely; to flutter.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Georgic III}}
#*: On his right shoulder his thick mane ', / '''Ruffles''' at speed, and dances in the wind.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be rough; to jar; to be in contention; hence, to put on airs; to swagger.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Henry 7}}
#*: They would '''ruffle''' with jurors.
#* {{RQ:Scott Peveril|passage=gallants who '''ruffled''' in silk and embroidery}}
# To make into a ruff; to draw or contract into puckers, plaits, or folds; to wrinkle.
# To erect in a ruff, as feathers.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson POA}}
#*: [The swan] '''ruffles''' her pure cold plume.
# {{lb|en|military}} To beat with the [[ruff]] or ruffle, as a drum.
# To throw together in a disorderly manner.
#* {{RQ:Chapman Odyssey}}
#*: I '''ruffled''' up fallen leaves in heap.

